  # The image and the two packages are pinned in stencil/pipeline.py, alongside
  # the browser image's. An unpinned prettier is the widest blast radius of the
  # three floating installs stn-s5b found: it rewrites every markdown file in
  # the package, so a major landing between two runs reformats a course's whole
  # source tree and buries the actual edit.
  #
  # Installed with `npm ci` against the lockfile beside this file, for the
  # reason Dockerfile.browser is (stn-5hv): naming two exact versions left
  # prettier's own dependencies floating, and prettier is the process that
  # rewrites the markdown. The manifest is written inline because it is derived
  # from the pins; the lockfile is shipped because only npm can compute it.
  #
  # THE SUBSHELL IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE. working_dir is /workspace, which is the
  # consumer's own package directory, and a course package legitimately holds a
  # package.json of its own. Running npm from there is asking which manifest it
  # resolves; running it from /tmp/fmt is not asking. The subshell keeps the
  # prettier invocation below in /workspace, where the `**/*.md` glob belongs.
  #
  # --no-config IS THE SAME QUESTION, ONE LOADER FURTHER ON (stn-20h). Putting
  # the install in /tmp/fmt answers which MANIFEST npm resolves. It leaves
  # prettier's own config discovery rooted in the mount: prettier searches
  # upward from each formatted file for .prettierrc, .prettierrc.json,
  # .prettierrc.cjs, prettier.config.js and package.json#prettier. Two of those
  # are JavaScript and are evaluated at load -- and ANY of them, the ones that
  # are pure data included, may carry a `plugins` entry that prettier then
  # requires out of the consumer's own node_modules. Measured on this image and
  # this prettier: both ran as uid 0, over the read-write mount, with the
  # network up, on every `make pkg`, and the build printed its usual success
  # output afterwards.
  #
  # So the service honours no configuration it did not bring with it. stencil
  # already dictated --prose-wrap and --print-width here and named its one
  # plugin by absolute path, so a consumer's config was only ever partly in
  # force; this makes "stencil's settings" true rather than nearly true. There
  # is deliberately no allowlist of "safe" config formats -- the dangerous file
  # in the second case above was the inert-looking one, and a `plugins` entry
  # is available in every format prettier reads.
  #
  # --no-editorconfig is NOT passed, and is not an oversight: measured,
  # --no-config already stops .editorconfig being consulted, so the second flag
  # would change nothing. Ignore files are untouched -- .prettierignore and
  # .gitignore still apply, they select which files are formatted rather than
  # deciding what code runs, and they are the escape hatch a consumer needs.
  #
  # THE LOCKFILE GUARD SAYS WHAT `cp: can't stat` DOES NOT. generate.py emits
  # the lockfile for every package it generates -- no predicate, because three
  # were tried and each left a case behind -- so a missing one now means a
  # package generated by an older stencil, or a file removed by hand. Both are
  # fixed by running `stencil gen`, and neither is obvious from the cp failure
  # this replaces.
  #
  # AND THE DIGEST SAYS WHAT A SUCCESSFUL `npm ci` DOES NOT (stn-qge). The two
  # guards above answer "is there a lockfile" and "which manifest does npm
  # resolve". Neither asks what the lockfile SAYS -- and this one is read out
  # of /workspace, the consumer's own directory. `npm ci` fetches whatever host
  # each `resolved` names and checks `integrity` against a value in that same
  # file, so a consumer-editable file chose which bytes became the prettier
  # this service then runs as uid 0 over that read-write mount. Neither
  # --ignore-scripts nor stn-20h's --no-config touches it: nothing has to run
  # at install time, because the payload runs when prettier runs. Measured
  # before the fix, with one `resolved` host changed and nothing else: npm
  # requested that host.
  #
  # So the entrypoint carries the sha256 of the lockfile `stencil gen` wrote --
  # rendered from the same bytes by pipeline.lockfile_digest, never written
  # down, so a re-vendor moves the file and the digest together -- and refuses
  # anything else. This is the stronger half of what tests/test_pins.py already
  # demands of stencil's own copy, where every `resolved` must be a
  # registry.npmjs.org URL for the package it claims to be: one hash covers
  # every URL, every integrity value, and everything else in the file at once.
  #
  # IT VERIFIES THE COPY, NOT THE ORIGINAL, and the order is the point. A check
  # before the `cp` hashes a file in a bind mount that the host can rewrite
  # between the check and the copy; a check after `npm ci` hashes a file npm
  # has already installed from. Hashing {{ format_tools_dir }}/package-lock.json
  # after the cp and before the install is the one arrangement where what was
  # hashed is what npm reads.
  #
  # The refusal says the file is stencil's, that editing it has no supported
  # effect, and that `stencil gen` restores it -- because the reader who hits
  # this has either edited it for a reason of their own or received a package
  # from someone who did, and "checksum mismatch" answers neither of them.
  #
  # WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLOSE is the compose file itself. A digest is a checksum,
  # not a signature: it proves two files in this directory agree with each
  # other, and both of them are files whoever edited the lockfile could edit.
  # Anyone who changes one and recomputes the other passes this check.
  #
  # It is still worth having, for two reasons that are worth being precise
  # about rather than overselling. It refuses every edit that touches only the
  # lockfile -- a script, a dependency bot, a half-finished hand edit, a bad
  # merge -- which is what a `resolved` URL in a generated artifact actually
  # attracts, because it reads as data rather than as a choice of what code to
  # run. And it turns the collusive case into a second thing to notice: the
  # digest on this line moves, in a file nobody hand-edits, without stencil
  # having been re-run. A reviewer who sees that has the tell; a reviewer
  # reading a changed `resolved` host in a 60-line JSON diff has to spot it.
  format-md:
    image: {{ node_image }}
    volumes:
      - .:/workspace:z
{% if has_package_output_dir %}
      # Build products live outside the package directory, which the mount at
      # /workspace cannot reach: an output directory that is a sibling of the
      # sources is `..` away, and `..` escapes a bind mount. Mounted separately.
      - {{ package_output_dir }}:/out:z
{% endif %}
    working_dir: /workspace
    environment:
      - NPM_CONFIG_UPDATE_NOTIFIER=false
    entrypoint:
      - sh
      - -c
      - |
        echo "Formatting markdown files..."
        if [ ! -f {{ format_lockfile_name }} ]; then \
          echo "format-md: {{ format_lockfile_name }} is not here." >&2; \
          echo "stencil writes it beside this compose file for every package it" >&2; \
          echo "generates. Run 'stencil gen' for this package. If it is still" >&2; \
          echo "missing afterwards, this compose file was generated by a stencil" >&2; \
          echo "older than the lockfile, or the file was removed by hand." >&2; \
          exit 1; \
        fi && \
        mkdir -p {{ format_tools_dir }} && \
        printf '%s\n' '{{ format_manifest }}' > {{ format_tools_dir }}/package.json && \
        cp {{ format_lockfile_name }} {{ format_tools_dir }}/package-lock.json && \
        if ! echo "{{ format_lockfile_digest }}  {{ format_tools_dir }}/package-lock.json" \
          | sha256sum -c >/dev/null 2>&1; then \
          echo "format-md: {{ format_lockfile_name }} is not the file stencil generated." >&2; \
          echo "That file is stencil's. It is the lockfile this service installs from," >&2; \
          echo "and editing it has no supported effect: a changed 'resolved' URL would" >&2; \
          echo "decide which bytes become the prettier that runs here, as root, over" >&2; \
          echo "this directory -- so the service refuses it rather than installing it." >&2; \
          echo "Run 'stencil gen' for this package to restore it." >&2; \
          exit 1; \
        fi && \
        (cd {{ format_tools_dir }} && npm ci --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund) && \
        {{ format_tools_dir }}/node_modules/.bin/prettier \
          --no-config \
          --plugin {{ format_tools_dir }}/node_modules/@awmottaz/prettier-plugin-void-html/prettier-plugin-void-html.js \
          --prose-wrap always --print-width 100 --write "**/*.md"
    restart: "no"

{# The argv comes from stencil/pipeline.py so that a test can assert on the
   same list this file builds with. Do not inline it back here. #}
  doc:
    image: {{ pandoc_image }}
    volumes:
      - .:/workspace:z
{% if has_package_output_dir %}
      # Build products live outside the package directory, which the mount at
      # /workspace cannot reach: an output directory that is a sibling of the
      # sources is `..` away, and `..` escapes a bind mount. Mounted separately.
      - {{ package_output_dir }}:/out:z
{% endif %}
    working_dir: /workspace
    entrypoint:
      - pandoc
{% for arg, why in pandoc_argv_doc %}
{% if why %}
{% for line in why.split('\n') %}
      # {{ line }}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
      - {{ arg }}
{% endfor %}
    restart: "no"

{% if has_slides %}
  slide:
    image: {{ pandoc_image }}
    volumes:
      - .:/workspace:z
{% if has_package_output_dir %}
      # Build products live outside the package directory, which the mount at
      # /workspace cannot reach: an output directory that is a sibling of the
      # sources is `..` away, and `..` escapes a bind mount. Mounted separately.
      - {{ package_output_dir }}:/out:z
{% endif %}
    working_dir: /workspace
    entrypoint:
      - pandoc
{% for arg, why in pandoc_argv_slide %}
{% if why %}
{% for line in why.split('\n') %}
      # {{ line }}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
      - {{ arg }}
{% endfor %}
    restart: "no"

{% endif %}
  # Prints an already-generated HTML file to PDF. This is a stage after the
  # doc/slide services rather than a second pandoc pipeline: the @page and
  # @media print rules the HTML already carries are the page geometry, so the
  # PDF and the browser's own Ctrl+P produce the same document.
  #
  # Invoked as: docker compose build pdf && docker compose run --rm pdf <in.html> <out.pdf>
  #
  # THE BUILD IS PART OF THE INVOCATION NOW, not a one-off setup step. The
  # driver script is baked into the image (see the entrypoint below), so a bare
  # `compose run` executes whichever html-to-pdf.js was baked the last time this
  # image was built -- and the image tag is shared by every package in the
  # config. `make pdf` already runs `$(DC) build pdf` first; this line is for
  # anyone typing the command by hand.
  pdf:
    build:
      context: .
      dockerfile: Dockerfile.browser
    image: localhost/{{ package_id }}_browser:latest
    volumes:
      - .:/workspace:z
{% if has_package_output_dir %}
      # Build products live outside the package directory, which the mount at
      # /workspace cannot reach: an output directory that is a sibling of the
      # sources is `..` away, and `..` escapes a bind mount. Mounted separately.
      - {{ package_output_dir }}:/out:z
{% endif %}
    # /workspace, EVEN THOUGH THE SCRIPT IS NOT HERE ANY MORE. The pair is the
    # design rather than a leftover: the Makefile passes `$(OUT)/Guide.html`,
    # which is relative for a package with no output_dir, so the process has to
    # START in the mount for a relative argument to mean anything.
    # html-to-pdf.js resolves both of its arguments against this directory and
    # then chdir()s out of it, before puppeteer can read a .puppeteerrc.cjs
    # from it (stn-jeq). Moving working_dir instead would have meant changing
    # what `make pdf` passes.
    working_dir: /workspace
    # The script is run from the image rather than from the mount, because Node
    # decides CommonJS-vs-ESM from the nearest package.json TO THE FILE, and at
    # /workspace that is the consumer's own -- `{"type": "module"}` there turned
    # this script into a parse error before any of its guards could speak
    # (stn-7ki). Dockerfile.browser COPYs it in from this same directory, so a
    # templates_dir override still reaches the image.
    entrypoint:
      - node
      - {{ browser_tools_dir }}/html-to-pdf.js
    restart: "no"

  # PDF/UA-1 (ISO 14289-1) conformance, which is a DIFFERENT STANDARD from the
  # WCAG 2.1 AA that check-access measures. pa11y reads the HTML; this reads
  # the PDF. A page can pass the first and produce a file that fails the
  # second -- <figure> wrappers are exactly that case, because HTML has no alt
  # on <figure> and WCAG has no rule asking for one.
  #
  # The image tag and the script both come from stencil/pipeline.py, so a test
  # runs the same text this file ships rather than an approximation of it. The
  # zero-file guard inside it is the reason it is a script: veraPDF exits 0
  # when it is given no files, so a check that ran too early would pass having
  # opened nothing. That rationale is written down at VERAPDF_SCRIPT.
  #
  # Every $ is doubled on the way in. Compose substitutes $VAR in a service
  # definition before the shell ever sees it, so an unescaped $found reaches
  # the script as the empty string and the guard silently stops guarding --
  # `[ "" -eq 0 ]` is not a comparison that fails safely. The escaping happens
  # here rather than in the constant so that pipeline.verapdf() can run the
  # same text directly through sh, where a doubled $ would be wrong.
  check-pdf:
    image: {{ verapdf_image }}
    volumes:
      - .:/workspace:z
{% if has_package_output_dir %}
      # Build products live outside the package directory, which the mount at
      # /workspace cannot reach: an output directory that is a sibling of the
      # sources is `..` away, and `..` escapes a bind mount. Mounted separately.
      - {{ package_output_dir }}:/out:z
{% endif %}
    working_dir: /workspace
    entrypoint:
      - sh
      - -c
      - |
{{ verapdf_script | replace('$', '$$') | indent(8, first=True) }}
      # $$0 for the script itself, so the filenames `make check-pdf` appends
      # land in "$$@". Without it the first filename would be swallowed as $$0
      # and silently go unchecked.
      - check-pdf
    restart: "no"

  # Shares the pdf service's image: both drive Chromium from Node, and building
  # one image for the pair keeps either from installing a browser at run time.
  check-access:
    build:
      context: .
      dockerfile: Dockerfile.browser
    image: localhost/{{ package_id }}_browser:latest
    volumes:
      - .:/workspace:z
{% if has_package_output_dir %}
      # Build products live outside the package directory, which the mount at
      # /workspace cannot reach: an output directory that is a sibling of the
      # sources is `..` away, and `..` escapes a bind mount. Mounted separately.
      - {{ package_output_dir }}:/out:z
{% endif %}
    working_dir: /workspace
    # The script comes from stencil/pipeline.py for the reason check-pdf's
    # does: a test runs the same text this file ships rather than a re-typed
    # approximation. It had to, in the end -- inlined here, the loop searched
    # /out and built its URL from /workspace, so check-access could not pass
    # at all for a package with an output_dir, and every test read this file's
    # text rather than running it.
    #
    # Every $ is doubled on the way in; compose substitutes $VAR in a service
    # definition before the shell sees it. The escaping happens here rather
    # than in the constant so the constant stays runnable directly through sh.
    entrypoint:
      - sh
      - -c
      - |
{{ check_access_script | replace('$', '$$') | indent(8, first=True) }}
      # $$0 for the script itself, then the directory to search as $$1. It is
      # absolute in both layouts, and the file:// URL is built from it, so
      # there is one path here rather than two that have to agree.
      - check-access
      - {% if has_package_output_dir %}/out{% else %}/workspace{% endif %}

    restart: "no"
